          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model (in theoretical physics)
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            on the largest length scales of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
          %p
            it is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} inflationary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+spacetime"} FRW spacetime
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"}> dark energy
            ”) and cold
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
              CDM concordance model
            (where “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            ” is the standard symbol for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            and “CDM” is for “cold dark matter”).
          %p
            The current model assumes that the energy density of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            consists of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                4.9%
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryon"} baryonic
                (visible)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            %li
              %p
                26.8%
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            %li
              %p
                68.3%
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"} dark energy
          %p
            %img{:src => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/DarkEnergyConcordance.gif", :width => "400"}/
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Einasto09"} Einasto 09, fig 17
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "https://ned.ipac.caltech.edu/level5/Sept09/Einasto/Einasto6.html"} here
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#structure_formation Structure formation
          %p
            Computer simulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+structure+formation"} cosmic structure formation
            on scales larger than that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/galaxies"} galaxies
            had always shown very good agreement of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                  %mi CDM
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda CDM
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            and observation.
          %p
            There used to be various discrepancies of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cold+dark+matter"} cold dark matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} models
            on the scale of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/galaxies"} galaxies
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "dark+matter#CuspCoreProblem"}> core-cusp behaviour
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/galaxy+rotation+curves"} galaxy rotation curves
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tully-Fisher+relation"} Tully-Fisher relation
          %p
            But recent analysis seems to show that more fine-grained analysis shows that
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cold+dark+matter"} cold dark matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} models
            match all of these observations well. See behind the above links for more.
          %h3#vacuum_energy_and_cosmological_constant Vacuum energy and Cosmological constant
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            freedom in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+gravity"}> perturbative quantum gravity
            ) induces freedom in the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+value"} vacuum expectation value
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress-energy+tensor"} stress-energy tensor
            and hence in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
          %p
            For review see
            %a{:href => "#Hack15"} Hack 15, section 3.2.1
          %p
            For more see at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "cosmological+constant#InPerturbativeQuantumGravity"} here
